&lt;h2&gt;&lt;em&gt;Please note: This screening will be held at the Kraft Center for Jewish Student Life, not at the IIJS offices.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Our spring film series concludes with &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Sabbath Queen&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, a powerful and intimate documentary. Please join us for an in-person screening, followed by a Q&amp;amp;A with the film&amp;rsquo;s subject, &lt;strong&gt;Rabbi Amichai Lau-Lavie&lt;/strong&gt;, and director &lt;strong&gt;Sandi DuBowski&lt;/strong&gt;. The screening will take place on &lt;strong&gt;Monday, April 27&lt;/strong&gt;, at &lt;strong&gt;6:00 p.m.&lt;/strong&gt;, at the &lt;strong&gt;Kraft Center for Jewish Student Life, Rennert Hall, 606 W. 115th St&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Co-sponsored by the School of the Arts, the Institute for the Study of Sexuality and Gender, and JQ.&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Filmed over 21 years by Sandi DuBowski, director of the groundbreaking documentary &lt;em&gt;Trembling Before G-d&lt;/em&gt;, this absorbing documentary follows Rabbi Amichai Lau-Lavie&amp;rsquo;s epic journey as the dynastic heir of 38 generations of Orthodox rabbis, including a Chief Rabbi of Israel. Torn between rejecting and embracing his lineage, Amichai becomes a drag queen rebel, a queer bio-dad, a founder of Lab/Shul&amp;mdash;an everybody-friendly, G-d-optional, artist-driven, experimental congregation in New York&amp;mdash;and, most unexpectedly, a rabbinical student. &lt;em&gt;Sabbath Queen&lt;/em&gt; profiles Amichai&amp;rsquo;s lifelong quest to cultivate Jewish community and reimagine the future of Judaism.&lt;br /&gt;
(105 minutes; English, Hebrew, &amp;amp; Yiddish with English subtitles)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Co-sponsored by Columbia University School of the Arts, Institute for the Study of Sexuality and Gender, and JQ&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Social activist and storyteller, writer and community leader, &lt;strong&gt;Rabbi Amichai Lau-Lavie&lt;/strong&gt; (he/him) is the Co-Founding Spiritual Leader of the Lab/Shul community in NYC and the creator of the ritual theater company Storahtelling, Inc. He was born in Israel and has been living in New York since 1998. He received his rabbinical ordination from the Jewish Theological Seminary of America in 2016, the 39th generation of rabbis in his family&amp;mdash;the first one to be openly queer.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sandi DuBowski&lt;/strong&gt; is an activist filmmaker.&amp;nbsp;His award-winning work, including &lt;em&gt;Trembling Before G-d&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;Sabbath Queen&lt;/em&gt;, has screened at the Sundance, Berlin, Tribeca and Toronto film festivals, has been theatrically released in 150 cities, and has been broadcast on ZDF/Arte, BBC, Channel 4, and PBS. Sandi has worked as the Outreach Director of Doc Society&amp;rsquo;s Good Pitch, nurturing other social justice documentaries, and is a co-founder of The Creative Resistance, a media collective responsible for award-winning political ads and design.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://youtu.be/M2zDJoIGASg" target="_blank"&gt;Trailer for &lt;em&gt;Sabbath Queen&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
